#NEXUS

[ lineage-network.nex -- heuristic phylogeny of English and its donor languages,
  used by the word-lineage skill as the map a word's path is read off.

  Mine: the tree, the dates and the branch lengths below are my reconstruction
  from recall of the standard Indo-European family tree. Branch lengths are in
  APPROXIMATE CENTURIES. They are a heuristic, not measured: no lexicostatistics,
  no cognate counts, no dating method stands behind them. Each node is pinned to
  a round date (shown in the node comments in the TREES block) and each branch
  length is (child date - parent date) / 100. Living languages are tips at
  2000 CE, so every living tip sits 55 centuries from the root (root = PIE,
  placed at c. 3500 BCE).

  Mine: internal nodes carry names (Old_English, Vulgar_Latin, ...). Several are
  unary (one child), because a stage like Old English -> Middle English ->
  Early Modern English is a chain of stages of one language, not a split. Unary
  nodes are legal extended Newick; some readers collapse them.

  Mine: Dutch (Old_Dutch -> Dutch) is my addition to the taxon set, so that the
  Dutch synonyms in the procedure and the Dutch trade edge (R5) have a place on
  the tree.

  Mine: Classical_Latin_Fossil (a zero-length tip off Latin) is also my
  addition. It stands for the frozen written classical standard, the donor
  node for the scholarly-Latin merge edge R3; it is not a living tip. ]

BEGIN TAXA;
	DIMENSIONS NTAX=13;
	TAXLABELS
		Old_Norse
		Modern_German
		Dutch
		British_Canadian_English
		American_English
		France_French
		Quebec_French
		Spanish
		Portuguese
		Italian
		Romanian
		Modern_Greek
		Classical_Latin_Fossil
	;
END;

BEGIN TREES;
	[ Branch lengths: approximate centuries. HEURISTIC, NOT MEASURED. ]
	[ Node dates (round, recall):
	    Proto_Indo_European  c. 3500 BCE   (root)
	    Proto_Germanic       c.  500 BCE   PIE -> PGmc       30
	    Old_Norse            c.  900 CE    PGmc -> ON        14   (tip; extinct stage)
	    West_Germanic        c.  200 CE    PGmc -> WGmc       7
	    Old_High_German      c.  750 CE    WGmc -> OHG        5.5
	    Modern_German        2000 CE       OHG -> ModG       12.5
	    Old_Dutch            c.  900 CE    WGmc -> ODu        7
	    Dutch                2000 CE       ODu -> Du         11
	    Old_English          c.  450 CE    WGmc -> OE         2.5
	    Middle_English       c. 1150 CE    OE -> ME           7
	    Early_Modern_English c. 1500 CE    ME -> EModE        3.5
	    English_1776         1776 CE       EModE -> split     2.76  (American/British split)
	    British_Canadian_Eng 2000 CE       split -> tip       2.24
	    American_English     2000 CE       split -> tip       2.24
	    Latin                c.  100 BCE   PIE -> Latin      34
	    Classical_Latin_Fossil c. 100 BCE  Latin -> tip       0     (the written classical/scholarly
	                                                                  standard, frozen; source of R3)
	    Vulgar_Latin         c.  300 CE    Latin -> VL        4
	    Old_French           c.  900 CE    VL -> OF           6
	    Modern_French        c. 1650 CE    OF -> ModF         7.5
	    France_French        2000 CE       ModF -> tip        3.5
	    Quebec_French        2000 CE       ModF -> tip        3.5   (New France settled 1600s)
	    Spanish, Portuguese, Italian, Romanian
	                         2000 CE       VL -> tip         17
	    Ancient_Greek        c.  400 BCE   PIE -> AGk        31
	    Modern_Greek         2000 CE       AGk -> tip        24 ]
	TREE lineage = [&R] ((Old_Norse:14,((Modern_German:12.5)Old_High_German:5.5,(Dutch:11)Old_Dutch:7,((((British_Canadian_English:2.24,American_English:2.24)English_1776:2.76)Early_Modern_English:3.5)Middle_English:7)Old_English:2.5)West_Germanic:7)Proto_Germanic:30,(Classical_Latin_Fossil:0,(((France_French:3.5,Quebec_French:3.5)Modern_French:7.5)Old_French:6,Spanish:17,Portuguese:17,Italian:17,Romanian:17)Vulgar_Latin:4)Latin:34,(Modern_Greek:24)Ancient_Greek:31)Proto_Indo_European;
END;

[ RETICULATIONS (merge edges). Mine. The tree above only splits; English did not
  only split, it took in whole layers from other branches. Each line below is one
  merge edge laid over the tree. Format:
    R#  donor node -> recipient node : dates : what came across (examples, recall)

  R0  Latin (church/Roman) -> Old_English        : c. 43-1066 CE (mostly 597 on)
        church and Roman-material words: wine, street, candle, bishop, school (recall)
        [Mine: not in the requested event list; added because step 6 of the
         procedure names the Roman/church Latin wave.]
  R1  Old_Norse -> Old_English                   : c. 800-1050 CE (Danelaw)
        core words, even pronouns: they, them, egg, sky, skin, take, give, die (recall)
  R2  Old_French (Norman/Anglo-Norman) -> Middle_English : 1066-1400 CE
        law, rule, church, cuisine, rank: court, judge, royal, beef, pork,
        commence (recall)
  R3  Classical_Latin_Fossil + Ancient_Greek -> Early_Modern_English : 1500-1700 CE
        scholarly and scientific coinages: initiate, incipient, data, genus,
        criterion, phenomenon, -ize/-ology formations (recall)
  R4  English_1776 -> {British_Canadian_English | American_English} : 1776 on
        the American/British split itself (a SPLIT edge, drawn in the tree as
        node English_1776). Divergent spelling (Webster 1828), vocabulary
        (fall/autumn, gotten), regional survivals (recall).
        Sub-edge: American_English -> British_Canadian_English (Canadian side),
        1783 on, United Empire Loyalist settlement carries American features into
        Canadian English (recall).
  R5  Colonial-era trade web                     : c. 1500-1900 CE
        among Spanish, Portuguese, French, Dutch and English, plus donors outside
        the tree. Mine: listed as external donors, not taxa.
          Portuguese -> English     : marmalade, molasses, veranda (recall)
          Spanish    -> English     : armada, mosquito, cargo; American: rodeo, ranch, canyon (recall)
          Dutch      -> English     : yacht, skipper, landscape; American: cookie, boss, Yankee (recall)
          French     -> English     : (continuing) etiquette, ballet, bureau (recall)
          Spanish <-> Portuguese    : shared Iberian trade and nautical vocabulary (recall)
          Malay   -(Portuguese/Dutch)-> English : amok, ketchup (via Malay/Hokkien), bamboo (recall)
          Tamil/Malayalam -(Portuguese)-> English : curry, mango, catamaran (recall)
          Hokkien Chinese -(Dutch)-> English : tea (te), ketchup (recall)
          Japanese -(Portuguese/Dutch, later direct)-> English : soy, tycoon (recall)
          Nahuatl  -(Spanish)-> English, French, Portuguese : tomato, chocolate, avocado (recall)
          Taino/Arawak -(Spanish)-> English : hurricane, hammock, canoe (recall)
          Arabic  -(Spanish/Italian)-> English : algebra, sugar, admiral, cotton (recall)
          Hindi/Urdu -> English (direct, British India) : shampoo, jungle, bungalow (recall)
  R6  British_Canadian_English <-> American_English : 1995 CE on
        internet-era re-convergence: shared online vocabulary, US spellings and
        terms spreading into British and Canadian usage, global English
        (recall). The tree splits at 1776 and merges back here.
  R7  English -> Modern_German, Dutch, France_French, others : 1800 CE on
        back-borrowing: German/Dutch "starten", French "le week-end", "le parking"
        (recall). Mine: added because the worked example (commence) needs it;
        an English word returns to the branch it descends from.
]
